React components often mount and unmount based on state. When a modal opens, you must trap focus. When it closes, return focus to the trigger.

Use `useEffect` to run focus code after render. For modals, you can use `react‑focus‑trap` or write a custom hook.

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  triggerRef.current?.focus();
}, [isOpen]);
```

Also, handle the `inert` attribute on the app root when a modal is open.

Buttons should respond to `onClick` (mouse) and `onKeyDown` (keyboard). React synthetic events work for both. Ensure custom interactions have keyboard equivalents.

Example: a `MenuItem` component should handle `onClick`, `onKeyDown` for Enter/Space, and Arrow keys for navigation (typically delegated to the menu container).

```jsx
<div
  role="menuitem"
  tabIndex={0}
  onClick={handleSelect}
  onKeyDown={(e) => { if (e.key === 'Enter') handleSelect(); }}
>
```
